{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794391106,"nonce":"/mw5wGgETMTfAIJAUiWUMEYoUE8=","title":"GPU animation P4: scroll, in three steps","message":"Part of the GPU-deferral plan (umbrella issue). 4a stands alone; 4b depends on P2; 4c depends on P1.\n\n**Today.** Scroll offset is applied as a paint-time translation baked into primitive bounds (`render_layer_with_motion` pushes `Transform::translate(get_scroll_offset)` after the clip). Any scroll invalidates the render cache and forces the slow path every frame: a full tree walk, a second walk to collect text, SVG and images, glyph preparation, batch rebuild and upload. The same offset, with sticky and fixed handling, is implemented four separate times: the paint walker, `collect_elements_recursive`, `EventRouter::hit_test_node` and `get_absolute_bounds`. Rounding already differs between them (`get_scroll_offset` rounds to device pixels, `get_absolute_bounds` does not).\n\n**4a: one scroll transform (CPU only, ships alone).** One function for the offset, sticky and fixed rules and the rounding policy, used by paint, collection, hit testing, accessibility and focus scrolling. Tests that all consumers agree. Also check two suspected faults: `cursor_inside_last_leaf` caches screen-space bounds with no invalidation on scroll, and hover is not refreshed after a scroll (neither confirmed), and whether `update_scroll_content_dimensions` re-clamps the offset when content shrinks (unconfirmed).\n\n**4b: scroll offset in the node table.** A scroll container is a table row; the CPU physics writes one offset pair per container per frame, instead of re-walking the tree. Content stays in the cached batch, emitted for the viewport plus an overscan band (the cull overscan is 200 px today); the CPU re-emits only when the offset leaves the band. Sticky and fixed become row flags solved in the shader. Text, SVG, images and clips are baked in screen space today, so clip transforms must become slot-aware. `ScrollPhysics` stays authoritative on the CPU: `ScrollRef`, `watch_y`, virtual lists, hit testing, accessibility and editor click mapping are unchanged. Culling is the risk: a GPU offset is only correct inside the emitted band.\n\n**4c: momentum as a descriptor.** A fling is a closed-form exponential decay; the final offset is known at release. During a fling the CPU evaluates the offset lazily when something reads it, and ticks per frame only while a subscriber needs a push (`watch_y`, virtual list). Bounce springs go through P1.\n\n**CSS (web standard).** `animation-timeline: scroll()` and `view()` (scroll-driven animations) give sticky headers, parallax, scrollbar fade and pull-to-refresh as ordinary tracks whose time source is a container's scroll progress. Also candidates: `scroll-behavior`, `overscroll-behavior`, `scroll-snap`, none of which exist today.\n\n**API.** `ScrollRef`, `ScrollPhysics`, `on_scroll` keep their meaning. New: the CSS properties above.\n\nAcceptance: 4a, all consumers agree on the offset in a test; 4b, a scroll frame no longer invalidates the cache or walks the tree (P0 benchmark), and the settled picture is pixel-identical to today's; 4c, a fling costs no per-frame CPU when nothing subscribes.","files":null}]}